  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Saturday)\nIssued at 1203 AM CDT SUN NOV 1 2026\n\nScattered, mainly showers, will move across Central Alabama for\nthe rest of tonight. This will be courtesy of shortwave activity\nfrom an upper trough extending from the Dakotas SEWD through the\nMid Mississippi River Valley and toward the Gulf Coast. As this\ntrough moves EWD across Eastern Conus, showers will become more\nwidespread on Sunday. While most of the activity will be rain\nshowers, a few isolated claps of thunder may be noted. This wave\nshould exit the region Monday morning with rain-free conditions\nto return for Monday afternoon through most of the day on Tuesday\nahead of the next surface frontal system.\n\nBy Tuesday night, our next system arrives as a low moves out of\nKansas and across to Illinois and Ohio by early Thursday. As it\ndoes so, it will drag a front across much of the Gulf Coast\nStates. We could see a few more thunderstorms with enough forcing\nfrom the front, along with some heavy rain at times. However,\nwith limiting factors, it is too early to tell if strong storms\nwill develop with the lower stream system. Behind it for Thursday\ninto Thursday night, some wrap around showers may occur as the \nupper low pushes across Eastern Conus. Then on Friday a second \n(rain-free) front is expected to bring down a colder Canadian air \nmass to lower the temperatures back into the 40s for lows for most\nof Central Alabama for next weekend.\n\n08",
